rl":"https://github.com/melon-1999/twenty/commit/d2e291d6a48322a29f8259c489ae48b03994c6e3","node_id":"C_kwDOTzfBtdoAKGQyZTI5MWQ2YTQ4MzIyYTI5ZjgyNTljNDg5YWU0OGIwMzk5NGM2ZTM","parents":[{"html_url":"https://github.com/melon-1999/twenty/commit/1f7050e917f894d86a5822c5743af81b437d86ec","sha":"1f7050e917f894d86a5822c5743af81b437d86ec","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/melon-1999/twenty/commits/1f7050e917f894d86a5822c5743af81b437d86ec"}],"sha":"d2e291d6a48322a29f8259c489ae48b03994c6e3","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/melon-1999/twenty/commits/d2e291d6a48322a29f8259c489ae48b03994c6e3"},{"author":{"avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/1834158?v=4","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Weiko/events{/privacy}","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Weiko/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Weiko/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Weiko/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"https://github.com/Weiko","id":1834158,"login":"Weiko","node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjE4MzQxNTg=","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Weiko/orgs","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Weiko/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Weiko/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Weiko/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Weiko/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/Weiko","user_view_type":"public"},"comments_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/melon-1999/twenty/commits/1f7050e917f894d86a5822c5743af81b437d86ec/comments","commit":{"author":{"date":"2026-09-10T23:12:04Z","email":"corentin@twenty.com","name":"Weiko"},"comment_count":0,"committer":{"date":"2026-09-10T23:12:04Z","email":"noreply@github.com","name":"GitHub"},"message":"Write pulled standalone page layout widgets as define files (#25743)\n\n## What\n\n`yarn twenty pull` now writes the standalone page layout widgets the\nserver exports since #25651: an entry of `manifest.pageLayoutWidgets`\nbecomes a `definePageLayoutWidget()` file. These are the widgets your\napplication placed on a tab it does not own, such as the Home tab of a\nstandard record page or a tab shipped by another application.\n\nUntil now they were exported by the server, written nowhere, and absent\nfrom the report, so a pull silently dropped them from the source tree.\n\n## How\n\nOne new pull entity kind, `pageLayoutWidget`, built the way navigation\nmenu items and standalone tabs are: the manifest entry is the file''s\nconfig, the file goes to\n`src/page-layout-widgets/<title>.page-layout-widget.ts`, which is the\nfolder `twenty dev add pageLayoutWidget` already uses, beside existing\nfiles of the same kind when there are any. A widget''s `title` is\nrequired by the manifest, so it names the file, and the collision rule\nqualifies it with the object the widget targets.\n\nThe enum bindings are the ones already used for widgets nested in a\nlayout: `WidgetType` on the widget, `PageLayoutTabLayoutMode` on its\nposition, and `AggregateOperations` and\n`ObjectRecordGroupByDateGranularity` inside chart configurations. The\ntab identifier is printed as the raw UUID like every other reference the\nwriters emit.\n\nThe configuration goes through the same `__typename` strip as a nested\nwidget, since a widget saved from the UI carries those GraphQL keys at\nevery level and a pulled file that kept them would fail the app''s own\ntypecheck.\n\nThe planner''s kind-to-manifest-key map and the base file guard learn the\ncollection. Nothing else in the pipeline needed touching:\n`TargetFunction.DefinePageLayoutWidget`,\n`ManifestEntityKey.PageLayoutWidgets` and the mapping between them\nalready existed, so the scanner recognises hand-written files of this\nkind.\n\n## Verification\n\n- A unit case pins the entity: the folder and suffix, the file named\nafter the title, the object as parent name, and the `__typename` strip.\n- The offline round-trip spec gains a standalone widget on the company\nrecord page tagline tab, so the written file is built by the real SDK\nand the collection is compared against the export. That case fails if\nthe kind is not written, because the rebuilt manifest carries no widget.\n- Full SDK unit suite, typecheck, lint and prettier.\n- Docs: the pull page lists the new kind under what it writes and drops\nit from the widgets it could not write, and the page layouts page says\nwhat pull writes back under `definePageLayoutWidget`.","tree":{"sha":"920d0b97009fbe2db728525a592116c613b162dd","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/melon-1999/twenty/git/trees/920d0b97009fbe2db728525a592116c613b162dd"},"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/melon-1999/twenty/git/commits/1f7050e917f894d86a5822c5743af81b437d86ec","verification":{"payload":"tree 920d0b97009fbe2db728525a592116c613b162dd\nparent 123f463eea7501ed4b55a24b2312f46fd58638d0\nauthor Weiko <corentin@twenty.com> 1789081924 +0000\ncommitter GitHub <noreply@github.com> 1789081924 +0000\n\nWrite pulled standalone page layout widgets as define files (#25743)\n\n## What\n\n`yarn twenty pull` now writes the standalone page layout widgets the\nserver exports since #25651: an entry of `manifest.pageLayoutWidgets`\nbecomes a `definePageLayoutWidget()` file. These are the widgets your\napplication placed on a tab it does not own, such as the Home tab of a\nstandard record page or a tab shipped by another application.\n\nUntil now they were exported by the server, written nowhere, and absent\nfrom the report, so a pull silently dropped them from the source tree.\n\n## How\n\nOne new pull entity kind, `pageLayoutWidget`, built the way navigation\nmenu items and standalone tabs are: the manifest entry is the file''s\nconfig, the file goes to\n`src/page-layout-widgets/<title>.page-layout-widget.ts`, which is the\nfolder `twenty dev add pageLayoutWidget` already uses, beside existing\nfiles of the same kind when there are any. A widget''s `title` is\nrequired by the manifest, so it names the file, and the collision rule\nqualifies it with the object the widget targets.\n\nThe enum bindings are the ones already used for widgets nested in a\nlayout: `WidgetType` on the widget, `PageLayoutTabLayoutMode` on its\nposition, and `AggregateOperations` and\n`ObjectRecordGroupByDateGranularity` inside chart configurations. The\ntab identifier is printed as the raw UUID like every other reference the\nwriters emit.\n\nThe configuration goes through the same `__typename` strip as a nested\nwidget, since a widget saved from the UI carries those GraphQL keys at\nevery level and a pulled file that kept them would fail the app''s own\ntypecheck.\n\nThe planner''s kind-to-manifest-key map and the base file guard learn the\ncollection. Nothing else in the pipeline needed touching:\n`TargetFunction.DefinePageLayoutWidget`,\n`ManifestEntityKey.PageLayoutWidgets` and the mapping between them\nalready existed, so the scanner recognises hand-written files of this\nkind.\n\n## Verification\n\n- A unit case pins the entity: the folder and suffix, the file named\nafter the title, the object as parent name, and the `__typename` strip.\n- The offline round-trip spec gains a standalone widget on the company\nrecord page tagline tab, so the written file is built by the real SDK\nand the collection is compared against the export. That case fails if\nthe kind is not written, because the rebuilt manifest carries no widget.\n- Full SDK unit suite, typecheck, lint and prettier.\n- Docs: the pull page lists the new kind under what it writes and drops\nit from the widgets it could not write, and the page layouts page says\nwhat pull writes back under `definePageLayoutWidget`.","reason":"valid","signature":"-----BEGIN PGP SIGNATURE-----\n\nwsFcBAABCAAQBQJqozlUCRC1aQ7uu5UhlAAAa9kQAAa01QTn/KzUcfDAYuat3rKJ\nio7C65a6La8jXLU1rxtcOKe7SP6vytFNp5vLyQ9UlL3XjKZVS7hLxT8QVGpCN9Cu\ngA6lEcMB8akehXksNJSuF8SZX4h5eNPZkmaYgO5TPuFJaZnGtlEuLRHkyslbyeUD\nRkVKAWc0C02HWUH