ca9","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/melon-1999/twenty/commits/b453166a78f164eaccea77cdf7e660b86d8bdca9"},{"author":{"avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/22936103?v=4","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/thomtrp/events{/privacy}","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/thomtrp/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/thomtrp/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/thomtrp/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"https://github.com/thomtrp","id":22936103,"login":"thomtrp","node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjIyOTM2MTAz","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/thomtrp/orgs","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/thomtrp/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/thomtrp/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/thomtrp/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/thomtrp/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/thomtrp","user_view_type":"public"},"comments_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/melon-1999/twenty/commits/0dc40c8764e07797b3b1b991a5ee52600449cc1f/comments","commit":{"author":{"date":"2026-09-10T14:09:46Z","email":"thomas.trompette@sfr.fr","name":"Thomas Trompette"},"comment_count":0,"committer":{"date":"2026-09-10T14:09:46Z","email":"noreply@github.com","name":"GitHub"},"message":"feat(workflow): add coreWorkflowId to core workflow versions (#25679)\n\n## Problem\n\n`core.\"workflowVersion\".\"workflowId\"` stores a **workspace** workflow\nid, not a core workflow id, and carries no FK. Verified against live\ndata: every row''s `workflowId` resolves in the workspace `workflow`\ntable, none in `core.\"workflow\"`. Consequences:\n\n- every core read that derives workflow statuses has to join back into\nthe per-workspace `workflow` table (`core-workflow-list.service.ts` does\nexactly that), and\n- the workspace `workflow` object can never be deleted, because core\nrows only make sense through it.\n\n## What this PR does (expand phase only)\n\nThis is the **expand** half of an expand-contract migration that\nre-points core workflow versions at core workflows:\n\n1. **New nullable `coreWorkflowId` uuid column** on\n`core.\"workflowVersion\"`, gated with `@WasIntroducedInUpgrade` pointing\nat the new 2.40 fast instance command. The gating matters: an ungated\nnew column crashes pre-upgrade instances the moment the new server build\nboots against a not-yet-migrated database (the readability column\nfollowed the same pattern for the same reason).\n2. **Fast instance command**\n(`2-40-instance-command-fast-1788960408161`) adds the column; `down`\ndrops it.\n3. **Workspace backfill command**\n(`upgrade:2-40:backfill-core-workflow-id-on-workflow-versions`) fills it\nfrom the existing mapping, per workspace, in one idempotent statement:\n\n   ```sql\n   UPDATE core.\"workflowVersion\" cv\n   SET \"coreWorkflowId\" = w.\"coreWorkflowId\"\n   FROM \"<schema>\".\"workflow\" w\n   WHERE cv.\"workspaceId\" = $1\n     AND cv.\"coreWorkflowId\" IS NULL\n     AND cv.\"workflowId\" = w.id\n     AND w.\"coreWorkflowId\" IS NOT NULL\n   ```\n\nSoft-deleted workspace workflows still contribute their mapping on\npurpose — a soft-deleted workflow still owns its versions and can be\nrestored. A workflow whose `coreWorkflowId` is null contributes nothing;\nwe never invent ids. Workspaces that never provisioned the workflow\nobject are skipped with the same object-not-found guard the 2-28 sibling\nuses. (Workspace commands have no `down` hook in the framework;\nreverting is `coreWorkflowId` back to NULL, which the instance command''s\n`down` covers by dropping the column.)\n4. **Dual-write in every live writer** of `core.\"workflowVersion\"`:\n- `upsertToCore` (repository upsert; also the path behind\n`recreateCoreVersionsByWorkflowId` and the committed 2-20 backfill\ncommand): batch-resolves the workspace `workflow` rows (`withDeleted` —\nsame soft-delete semantics as the backfill) and stamps each core row.\nWhen resolution comes back null for a version that links an existing\nowned core row, the row''s current `coreWorkflowId` is carried over\ninstead of being overwritten with null — same preserve-once-linked\nsemantics as the raw path below.\n- `mirrorWorkflowVersionWrite` (raw transactional INSERT ... ON\nCONFLICT; the path behind `writeWorkflowVersionAndMirror` and the\nactivation flow): resolves the workflow inside the caller''s transaction\nand writes the column. On conflict it uses\n`COALESCE(EXCLUDED.\"coreWorkflowId\", existing)` so a resolution that\ncomes back null (workflow mirror not yet linked) can never null out a\nvalue that a previous write or the backfill already set — the\nworkflowId→coreWorkflowId mapping is immutable once established.\n- `prefillWorkflows` (workspace provisioning seeds Quick Lead / Create\ncompany, including their core version rows): stamps the deterministic\ncore workflow ids on the seeded core version rows, so workspaces created\nafter the upgrade don''t depend on the one-time backfill.\n\nNo column-availability guard is used. `@WasIntroducedInUpgrade` on the\nentity is the only gating, and it was verified not to cover this case:\non an instance whose 2.40 slow commands are already recorded, the\nupgrade cursor sits past a fast command inserted afterwards, so the\ncolumn is treated as applied and the write fails with `column does not\nexist`. A hand-rolled probe reads the same state and behaves\nidentically, so it was removed rather than kept as false protection. The\nexposure is the window between the new build serving traffic and\n`run-instance-commands` completing.\n\nPlacing the resolution in the shared mirror path is deliberate: #25626\n(`createInitialDraftVersionForWorkflow`) flows through\n`writeWorkflowVersionAndMirror`, so it picks up the dual-write\nautomatically after merge with no changes on that branch.\n\nThe committed 2-20/2-22 backfill commands also insert core version rows,\nbut committed upgrade command logic must not be rewritten; rows they\ncreated on already-upgraded instances are covered by the new backfill\ncommand instead.\n\n## Known expand-phase gaps (accepted)\n\n- A brand-new workflow''s initial draft version can be mirrored before\nthe workflow''s own core mirror writes `coreWorkflowId` back onto the\nworkspace row; that core version row then starts with a null\n`coreWorkflowId`. It converges on the next version write (every builder\nedit and activation re-mirrors, and the ON CONFLICT clause fills nulls\nin).\n- A pod that booted before the upgrade keeps the column hidden until the\nupgrade runs in-process or the pod restarts\n(`UpgradeAwareEntityMetadataAdapter.refresh()` semantics); rows it\nwrites in that window stay null. Workspaces provisioned in the window\nare still covered when the workspace backfill command iterates them\nduring the upgrade.\n- Both gaps are invisible today (no reader consumes the column) and the\ncontract phase re-runs a backfill before any reader flips over.\n\n## Explicitly deferred to the contract phase\n\n- No reader changes — status derivation still joins the workspace\n`workflow` table.\n- No FK to `core.\"workflow\"`.\n- No index changes (including moving\n`IDX_WORKFLOW_VERSION_ONE_ACTIVE_PER_WORKFLOW` off the workspace-scoped\n`workflowId`).\n- No rename of `workflowId` (`@WasRenamedInUpgrade` comes with the\ncontract).\n\n## Tests\n\n- Unit spec for the backfill command (mirrors the 2-28 sibling''s spec):\nupdate shape and predicates, no `deletedAt` filter, dry-run, no-op,\nunprovisioned-workspace skip.\n- Integration spec\n(`workflow-version-core-workflow-id.integration-spec.ts`): creates a\nworkflow via the workspace API, waits for the workflow mirror link,\nperforms a version write, and asserts at the database level that the\ncore version row''s `coreWorkflowId` points at an actual\n`core.\"workflow\"` row. No GraphQL assertion is possible by design — no\nreader exposes the column in this phase.\n\n## Interaction with #25626\n\n#25626 merged after this branch was cut and adds\n`createInitialDraftVersionForWorkflow`, a new writer of\n`core.\"workflowVersion\"`. It routes through\n`writeWorkflowVersionAndMirror` → `mirrorWorkflowVersionWrite`, which is\nthe mirrored INSERT this PR extends, so the new create path populates\n`coreWorkflowId` without further changes. That is the reason the\ndual-write was placed on the shared mirror path rather than at each call\nsite.","tree":{"sha":"64b2d2c5a412655aaf4e4f16452ac7a2f0b5d4a0","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/melon-1999/twenty/git/trees/64b2d2c5a412655aaf4e4f16452ac7a2f0b5d4a0"},"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/melon-1999/twenty/git/commits/0dc40c8764e07797b3b1b991a5ee52600449cc1f","verification":{"payload":"tree 64b2d2c5a412655aaf4e4f16452ac7a2f0b5d4a0\nparent 8bcc1c8120e46031a5b4211440dd9462005ac3bc\nauthor Thomas Trompette <thomas.trompette@sfr.fr> 1789049386 +0000\ncommitter GitHub <noreply@github.com> 1789049386 +0000\n\nfeat(workflow): add coreWorkflowId to core workflow versions (#25679)\n\n## Problem\n\n`core.\"workflowVersion\".\"workflowId\"` stores a **workspace** workflow\nid, not a core workflow id, and carries no FK. Verified against live\ndata: every row''s `workflowId` resolves in the workspace `workflow`\ntable, none in `core.\"workflow\"`. Consequences:\n\n- every core read that derives workflow statuses has to join back into\nthe per-workspace `workflow` table (`core-workflow-list.service.ts` does\nexactly that), and\n- the workspace `workflow` object can never be deleted, because core\nrows only make sense through it.\n\n## What this PR does (expand phase only)\n\nThis is the **expand** half of an expand-contract migration that\nre-points core workflow versions at core workflows:\n\n1. **New nullable `coreWorkflowId` uuid column** on\n`core.\"workflowVersion\"`, gated with `@WasIntroducedInUpgrade` pointing\nat the new 2.40 fast instance command. The gating matters: an ungated\nnew column crashes pre-upgrade instances the moment the new server build\nboots against a not-yet-migrated database (the readability column\nfollowed the same pattern for the same reason).\n2. **Fast instance command**\n(`2-40-instance-command-fast-1788960408161`) adds the column; `down`\ndrops it.\n3. **Workspace backfill command**\n(`upgrade:2-40:backfill-core-workflow-id-on-workflow-versions`) fills it\nfrom the existing mapping, per workspace, in one idempotent statement:\n\n   ```sql\n   UPDATE core.\"workflowVersion\" cv\n   SET \"coreWorkflowId\" = w.\"coreWorkflowId\"\n   FROM \"<schema>\".\"workflow\" w\n   WHERE cv.\"workspaceId\" = $1\n     AND cv.\"coreWorkflowId\" IS NULL\n     AND cv.\"workflowId\" = w.id\n     AND w.\"coreWorkflowId\" IS NOT NULL\n   ```\n\nSoft-deleted workspace workflows still contribute their mapping on\npurpose — a soft-deleted workflow still owns its versions and can be\nrestored. A workflow whose `coreWorkflowId` is null contributes nothing;\nwe never invent ids. Workspaces that never provisioned the workflow\nobject are skipped with the same object-not-found guard the 2-28 sibling\nuses. (Workspace commands have no `down` hook in the framework;\nreverting is `coreWorkflowId` back to NULL, which the instance command''s\n`down` covers by dropping the column.)\n4. **Dual-write in every live writer** of `core.\"workflowVersion\"`:\n- `upsertToCore` (repository upsert; also the path behind\n`recreateCoreVersionsByWorkflowId` and the committed 2-20 backfill\ncommand): batch-resolves the workspace `workflow` rows (`withDeleted` —\nsame soft-delete semantics as the backfill) and stamps each core row.\nWhen resolution comes back null for a version that links an existing\nowned core row, the row''s current `coreWorkflowId` is carried over\ninstead of being overwritten with null — same preserve-once-linked\nsemantics as the raw path below.\n- `mirrorWorkflowVersionWrite` (raw transactional INSERT ... ON\nCONFLICT; the path behind `writeWorkflowVersionAndMirror` and the\nactivation flow): resolves the workflow inside the caller''s transaction\nand writes the column. On conflict it uses\n`COALESCE(EXCLUDED.\"coreWorkflowId\", existing)` so a resolution that\ncomes back null (workflow mirror not yet linked) can never null out a\nvalue that a previous write or the backfill already set — the\nworkflowId→coreWorkflowId mapping is immutable once established.\n- `prefillWorkflows` (workspace provisioning seeds Quick Lead / Create\ncompany, including their core version rows): stamps the deterministic\ncore workflow ids on the seeded core version rows, so workspaces created\nafter the upgrade don''t depend on the one-time backfill.\n\nNo column-availability guard is used. `@WasIntroducedInUpgrade` on the\nentity is the only gating, and it was verified not to cover this case:\non an instance whose 2.40 slow commands are already recorded, the\nupgrade cursor sits past a fast command inserted afterwards, so the\ncolumn is treated as applied and the write fails with `column does not\nexist`. A hand-rolled probe reads the same state and behaves\nidentically, so it was removed rather than kept as false protection. The\nexposure is the window between the new build serving traffic and\n`run-instance-commands` completing.\n\nPlacing the resolution in the shared mirror path is deliberate: #25626\n(`createInitialDraftVersionForWorkflow`) flows through\n`writeWorkflowVersionAndMirror`, so it picks up the dual-write\nautomatically after merge with no changes on that branch.\n\nThe committed 2-20/2-22 backfill commands also insert core version rows,\nbut committed upgrade command logic must not be rewritten; rows they\ncreated on already-upgraded instances are covered by the new backfill\ncommand instead.\n\n## Known expand-phase gaps (accepted)\n\n- A brand-new workflow''s initial draft version can be mirrored before\nthe workflow''s own core mirror writes `coreWorkflowId` back onto the\nworkspace row; that core version row then starts with a null\n`coreWorkflowId`. It converges on the next version write (every builder\nedit and activation re-mirrors, and the ON CONFLICT clause fills nulls\nin).\n- A pod that booted before the upgrade keeps the column hidden until the\nupgrade runs in-process or the pod restarts\n(`UpgradeAwareEntityMetadataAdapter.refresh()` semantics); rows it\nwrites in that window stay null. Workspaces provisioned in the window\nare still covered when the workspace backfill command iterates them\nduring the upgrade.\n- Both gaps are invisible today (no reader consumes the column) and the\ncontract phase re-runs a backfill before any reader flips over.\n\n## Explicitly deferred to the contract phase\n\n- No reader changes — status derivation still joins the workspace\n`workflow` table.\n- No FK to `core.\"workflow\"`.\n- No index changes (including moving\n`IDX_WORKFLOW_VERSION_ONE_ACTIVE_PER_WORKFLOW` off the workspace-scoped\n`workflowId`).\n- No rename of `workflowId` (`@WasRenamedInUpgrade` comes with the\ncontract).\n\n## Tests\n\n- Unit spec for the backfill command (mirrors the 2-28 sibling''s spec):\nupdate shape and predicates, no `deletedAt` filter, dry-run, no-op,\nunprovisioned-workspace skip.\n- Integration spec\n(`workflow-version-core-workflow-id.integration-spec.ts`): creates a\nworkflow via the workspace API, waits for the workflow mirror link,\nperforms a version write, and asserts at the database level that the\ncore version row''s `coreWorkflowId` points at an actual\n`core.\"workflow\"` row. No GraphQL assertion is possible by design — no\nreader exposes the column in this phase.\n\n## Interaction with #25626\n\n#25626 merged after this branch was cut and adds\n`createInitialDraftVersionForWorkflow`, a new writer of\n`core.\"workflowVersion\"`. It routes through\n`writeWorkflowVersionAndMirror` → `mirrorWorkflowVersionWrite`, which is\nthe mirrored INSERT this PR extends, so the new create path populates\n`coreWorkflowId` without further changes. That is the reason the\ndual-write was placed on the shared mirror path rather than at each call\nsite.","reason":"valid","signature":"-----BEGIN PGP SIGNATURE-----\n\nwsFcBAABCAAQBQJqoro6CRC1aQ7uu5UhlAAAMPQQAKbNMaxPeUHtNnKx7Kg0nIaI\nXVgBZTibnlLyyC1x1S49gHuK8h2rhlJ0DoLy2h+HLKG3xoOksgZC/hunOOCsx2XK\nx/69pkQmjdCScZCotShZvbKKa/u8PtfRPCb1jCo4jxeOrjX1Z6XwBqWjZNid+FYZ\nVyJpmTQ5jPh/Nry37rWaai+jxmr6eEswUcG8FfsFGUZJtRFpXpUQgpKPYN64kXGV\n4b3+acQOsWy01frdM34NkjSuCzI6sbFOTHQfqPm2IQB4BceMu1KvT+rKWliIxA1+\nfkhnHhCNZSgzqMGsilDrRWxLbLZMlvmzqwqpqycof1gEEWd3hqjasUV0O2b0IluN\nErAoC77JNpExnPUfLh9aufC3g60NPDBumUh/KszNed64jP5NWoINqYFpfJIR+kJO\nJrF8D+O5knv2bqmMIujkZzV21kS26jNB9mHQlvDAvojxJL9ezSyvEUBZZfY+6f26\nWTgupJp5h7cCSBmZjj5yv/1gk5D190aDtR+s/FRB7gdbcPOLGV8CIZKtkh7P9UN9\n1c4+khRPE6AZ7IcBdsTkrr8IdTZrBVwcwhPN/L6bdQAE4NpjFR0n8MNv52KOZwQt\nPqxz4CTh4W2soB/jaG5mBR+EPvFBUuYR80wwvD24nlRI4UIbPJ/wBS1fuRY5KR6f\nCb8XOfzhtujsOKTBsOtn\n=t315\n-----END PGP SIGNATURE-----\n","verified":true,"verified_at":"2026-09-12T09:06:20Z"}},"committer":{"avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/19864447?v=4","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/events{/privacy}","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"https://github.com/web-flow","id":19864447,"login":"web-flow","node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjE5ODY0NDQ3","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/orgs","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow","user_view_type":"public"},"html_url":"https://github.com/melon-1999/twenty/commit/0dc40c8764e07797b3b1b991a5ee52600449cc1f","node_id":"C_kwDOTzfBtdoAKDBkYzQwYzg3NjRlMDc3OTdiM2IxYjk5MWE1ZWU1MjYwMDQ0OWNjMWY","parents":[{"html_url":"https://github.com/melon-1999/twenty/commit/8bcc1c8120e46031a5b4211440dd9462005ac3bc","sha":"8bcc1c8120e46031a5b4211440dd9462005ac3bc","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/melon-1999/twenty/commits/8bcc1c8120e46031a5b4211440dd9462005ac3bc"}],"sha":"0dc40c8764e07797b3b1b991a5ee52600449cc1f","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/melon-1999/twenty/commits/0dc40c8764e07797b3b1b991a5ee52600449cc1f"},{"author":{"avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/71827178?v=4","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/bosiraphael/events{/privacy}","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/bosiraphael/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/bosiraphael/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/bosiraphael/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"https://github.com/bosiraphael","id":71827178,"login":"bosiraphael","node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjcxODI3MTc4","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/bosiraphael/orgs","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/bosiraphael/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/bosiraphael/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/bosiraphael/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/bosiraphael/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/bosiraphael","user_view_type":"public"},"comments_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/melon-1999/twenty/commits/8bcc1c8120e46031a5b4211440dd9462005ac3bc/comments","commit":{"author":{"date":"2026-09-10T14:02:30Z","email":"71827178+bosiraphael@users.noreply.github.com","name":"Raphaël Bosi"},"comment_count":0,"committer":{"date":"2026-09-10T14:02:30Z","email":"noreply@github.com","name":"GitHub"},"message":"Migrate twenty-ui Slider to Base UI (#25716)\n\nReplace the native-range Slider wrapper with a Base UI compound:\n`Slider.Root`, `Control`, `Track`, `Indicator`, `Thumb`, `Label`, and\n`Value`. The new API supports controlled and uncontrolled values,\nranges, vertical orientation, RTL, and native props/ref/render on each\npart.\n\nMigrate credit selection, audio seeking, and the renderer gallery\ntogether. Audio dragging previews the position through `onValueChange`\nand seeks on `onValueCommitted`; keyboard changes seek immediately.\nCredit selection uses numeric callbacks, retains its green appearance\nthrough `color=\"success\"`, and disables the control when fewer than two\npackages are available. Remove the legacy prop types.\n\nUpdate the transcript integration story to drive pointer dragging\nthrough the compound Slider. Its previous synthetic input event\ntriggered Base UI''s immediate-commit behavior instead of simulating a\ndrag. Preserve preview, release, keyboard, and playback assertions, with\ntime for Chromium to buffer audio before checking playback progress.\nGive standalone audio-player stories a fixed-width wrapper and the\nstandard component decorator so Argos fit-to-content screenshots cannot\ncollapse the slider track.\n\nValidation:\n- 682 UI unit tests pass, including 76 new Slider conformance checks.\nOne shared aria-attribute case is skipped for Thumb because Base UI\nforwards the accessible name to its nested range input; stories verify\nthat name.\n- All 15 Slider and 18 audio-player/transcript-body Chromium stories\npass, covering keyboard and pointer changes, controlled state,\ncancellation, form submission, ranges, RTL, seeking, and playback. The\n18 frontend stories pass with retries disabled, including Argos\nscreenshot checks. All four audio-player stories also pass with\nfit-to-content sizing applied before rendering.\n- Direct UI, frontend, and renderer typechecks, changed-file lint/format\nchecks, the UI build, size limits, and package-size checks pass.\n- Light/dark catalogs, vertical and RTL layouts, and mobile keyboard\nfocus were visually checked. Catalog stories use the shared\ncolor-contrast deferral for catalog labels.","tree":{"sha":"c9c59f6d34c8f4272c694ee35bb406958044ec44","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/melon-1999/twenty/git/trees/c9c59f6d34c8f4272c694ee35bb406958044ec44"},"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/melon-1999/twenty/git/commits/8bcc1c8120e46031a5b4211440dd9462005ac3bc","verification":{"payload":"tree c9c59f6d34c8f4272c694ee35bb406958044ec44\nparent 1732736aa637835a0167cf4df2ae43727a789500\nauthor Raphaël Bosi <71827178+bosiraphael@users.noreply.github.com> 1789048950 +0000\ncommitter GitHub <noreply@github.com> 1789048950 +0000\n\nMigrate twenty-ui Slider to Base UI (#25716)\n\nReplace the native-range Slider wrapper with a Base UI compound:\n`Slider.Root`, `Control`, `Track`, `Indicator`, `Thumb`, `Label`, and\n`Value`. The new API supports controlled and uncontrolled values,\nranges, vertical orientation, RTL, and native props/ref/render on each\npart.\n\nMigrate credit selection, audio seeking, and the renderer gallery\ntogether. Audio dragging previews the position through `onValueChange`\nand seeks on `onValueCommitted`; keyboard changes seek immediately.\nCredit selection uses numeric callbacks, retains its green appearance\nthrough `color=\"success\"`, and disables the control when fewer than two\npackages are available. Remove the legacy prop types.\n\nUpdate the transcript integration story to drive pointer dragging\nthrough the compound Slider. Its previous synthetic input event\ntriggered Base UI''s immediate-commit behavior instead of simulating a\ndrag. Preserve preview, release, keyboard, and playback assertions, with\ntime for Chromium to buffer audio before checking playback progress.\nGive standalone audio-player stories a fixed-width wrapper and the\nstandard component decorator so Argos fit-to-content screenshots cannot\ncollapse the slider track.\n\nValidation:\n- 682 UI unit tests pass, including 76 new Slider conformance checks.\nOne shared aria-attribute case is skipped for Thumb because Base UI\nforwards the accessible name to its nested range input; stories verify\nthat name.\n- All 15 Slider and 18 audio-player/transcript-body Chromium stories\npass, covering keyboard and pointer changes, controlled state,\ncancellation, form submission, ranges, RTL, seeking, and playback. The\n18 frontend stories pass with retries disabled, including Argos\nscreenshot checks. All four audio-player stories also pass with\nfit-to-content sizing applied before rendering.\n- Direct UI, frontend, and renderer typechecks, changed-file lint/format\nchecks, the UI build, size limits, and package-size checks pass.\n- Light/dark catalogs, vertical and RTL layouts, and mobile keyboard\nfocus were visually checked. Catalog stories use the shared\ncolor-contrast deferral for catalog labels.","reason":"valid","signature":"-----BEGIN PGP SIGNATURE-----\n\nwsFcBAABCAAQBQJqoriGCRC1aQ7uu5UhlAAASfwQAIO+fn7imjgghl3GkSg7yhC6\nLi/4B6H2FJ6nsLRZdgVr8j/tQSzjT3cA2UXYOI/o29IGGBiI1kDbIG0fsGIIMWPz\n+roq84CTMhaBJ3Mq1abfN9IX771Ei1hYlfZqLx9F7vuYQsWUv39OtAvM84wdGfUe\nS1dfcRal5eBVe6s95hu4ihjcQuhit/1ksWcxw6sbHySXOJgFVSFDwExMarCkfaI+\nAeEL2g+ZvQ0zI6c5dsN/r7ldwZ0FkPUsbJ2YFjZbKIVF5RnrCxPPDXGqIifsgXtb\ni33nBVFaECJWLEAEino+Gp+pGW0pW3f0/OsAMcpPbPW2cUXg8YAGk7S1BAHZv7yl\nihkbxR41DGCUAM3Eqd7t1HBTSmwdIoQe6f+MdwaPE8liLppIzkWayeo8VioB86F1\nmTeeedmstb5yMNZH/elZnER7jiuMUGEzHKhcUwJVVb61+2pQNefzeWiXJFx+TfBx\nSQLCIzXWZpMHHJMXtjbUhzsiZXoNTBmD27wW712X+LlTgUQiQc8yfE3CQ0hr+B9M\nUphgfdqCEVz351GsYemYfX3qtpdNKDzyM8ZDT8YRhUHM83ZiUd31Xgsuw+4KgkB7\ncOG3Bkb33QwTn7aExl698cr6NFXYftm+/uCNlHTcCN95hd+UVuAdi9LN70vT/iu2\ntzRC3ZRSoVlwtNTBHlov\n=guQn\n-----END PGP SIGNATURE-----\n","verified":true,"verified_at":"2026-09-12T09:06:20Z"}},"committer":{"avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/19864447?v=4","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/events{/privacy}","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"https://github.com/web-flow","id":19864447,"login":"web-flow","node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjE5ODY0NDQ3","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/orgs","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow","user_view_type":"public"},"html_url":"https://github.com/melon-1999/twenty/commit/8bcc1c8120e46031a5b4211440dd9462005ac3bc","node_id":"C_kwDOTzfBtdoAKDhiY2MxYzgxMjBlNDYwMzFhNWI0MjExNDQwZGQ5NDYyMDA1YWMzYmM","parents":[{"html_url":"https://github.com/melon-1999/twenty/commit/1732736aa637835a0167cf4df2ae43727a789500","sha":"1732736aa637835a0167cf4df2ae43727a789500","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/melon-1999/twenty/commits/1732736aa637835a0167cf4df2ae43727a789500"}],"sha":"8bcc1c8120e46031a5b4211440dd9462005ac3bc","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/melon-1999/twenty/commits/8bcc1c8120e46031a5b4211440dd9462005ac3bc"},{"author":{"avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/81605929?v=4","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/abdulrahmancodes/events{/privacy}","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/abdulrahmancodes/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/abdulrahmancodes/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/abdulrahmancodes/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"https://github.com/abdulrahmancodes","id":81605929,"login":"abdulrahmancodes","node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjgxNjA1OTI5","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/abdulrahmancodes/orgs","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/abdulrahmancodes/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/abdulrahmancodes/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/abdulrahmancodes/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/abdulrahmancodes/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/abdulrahmancodes","user_view_type":"public"},"comments_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/melon-1999/twenty/commits/1732736aa637835a0167cf4df2ae43727a789500/comments","commit":{"author":{"date":"2026-09-10T13:54:13Z","email":"81605929+abdulrahmancodes@users.noreply.github.com","name":"Abdul Rahman"},"comment_count":0,"committer":{"date":"2026-09-10T13:54:13Z","email":"noreply@github.com","name":"GitHub"},"message":"feat(slack): answer the assistant request in the execution that records it (#25702)\n\n## Why\n\nIn production the Slack assistant can take minutes to answer, and the\nSlack Assistant Request record itself shows up late. The record is\ncreated by the queued enqueue step, so the time is lost in queue waits,\nnot in the assistant code. Until now a message went through three queued\nexecutions:\n\n1. the resolver, run inline by the server route;\n2. the enqueue function, one job on the logic function queue, which\ncreates the record;\n3. the worker, triggered by the record''s `created` event: one job on the\ntrigger queue (concurrency 1 per pod, fed by every record event of every\nworkspace), then another job on the logic function queue, waiting behind\nevery database-event trigger of every app.\n\n#25701 makes the route dispatch job in step 2 jump the fan-out. This PR\nremoves step 3 from the answer path.\n\n## What\n\n- `slack-events-enqueue` becomes `slack-assistant-request` (same\nuniversal identifier, so installs upgrade in place). It records the\nrequest as before and, when it has a resumable record, answers it in the\nsame execution. Its timeout is the worker''s 4 minute budget, and the\nagent deadline is computed at function entry so the failure-reply\nreserve is measured against the enclosing timeout.\n- The worker''s handler moves to\n`handlers/slack-assistant-worker-handler.ts` and takes the record\ndirectly instead of a database event.\n- `slack-assistant-worker` and its `created` trigger stay for one\nrelease as an upgrade drain. Events still waiting in the trigger backlog\nand worker jobs already emitted at upgrade time refer to that trigger\nand function; without them those requests would stay PENDING with no\nSlack redelivery. The drain hands the created record to the claim-based\nhandler, so a request already answered inline loses the claim and exits\nwith one cheap update. Remove the drain in the release after this one.\n- Retries resume unfinished work. A record found still PENDING is handed\nback to the worker rather than skipped, so an execution that died after\nwriting the record is recovered by the queue retry or a Slack\nredelivery. The loser of a racing create fetches the winning row and\nmakes the same decision.\n- A record killed mid-answer is recovered by a lease. A PROCESSING\nrecord whose `updatedAt` is older than the function timeout has no live\nowner (no execution outlives its timeout), so it is treated as\nresumable. The worker claims the record with one conditional update\n(`updateSlackAssistantRequests` filtered on `status = PENDING` or\n`status = PROCESSING and updatedAt < now - timeout`) and only proceeds\nif it won the row, so a redelivery racing a live execution can never\nproduce a second answer, while one that lands after the execution died\ntakes it over. The remaining window is a Slack post that succeeded\nwithout its DONE write followed by a retry more than 4 minutes later,\nwhich yields a duplicate answer rather than a lost one.\n- Trade-off made explicit: after #25701 the route dispatch job runs at\npriority 1, and this PR makes that job own the full 4 minute agent\nbudget rather than a short enqueue. Slack assistant runs therefore jump\nevery database-event trigger on the shared queue for the whole answer.\nThat is the intended choice while Slack volume is small next to the\nfan-out. The cleaner long-term seam is a \"user is waiting\" priority on\nthe SDK''s `enqueueJobs` or a separately concurrency-controlled\ninteractive lane on the server; `enqueueJobs` today enqueues at priority\n10, worse than the fan-out, which is why it was not used as the hand-off\nhere.\n- App version 1.0.2 to 1.1.0.\n\n## Test plan\n\n- `yarn typecheck`, `yarn lint` and `yarn test:unit` in\n`packages/twenty-apps/public/slack` are clean (687 tests, 101 files).\nUnit tests cover the request function''s hand-off and entry-time\ndeadline, the enqueue step''s create, resume, stale take-over,\ncreate-race and skip branches, the worker''s claim, lost-claim and lease\npaths, the lease predicate itself, and the drain''s delegation to the\nclaim-based handler.\n- The worker integration suite creates PENDING records so the claim runs\nagainst the live server; the enqueue integration tests assert the\nreturned record and the resume on redelivery. Both run in the\ntwenty-apps CI job.","tree":{"sha":"98afabc093eff3c04f2b6f0410d2c75ca81fb512","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/melon-1999/twenty/git/trees/98afabc093eff3c04f2b6f0410d2c75ca81fb512"},"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/melon-1999/twenty/git/commits/1732736aa637835a0167cf4df2ae43727a789500","verification":{"payload":"tree 98afabc093eff3c04f2b6f0410d2c75ca81fb512\nparent fa9870536abce97ef2cb9622d9d1941443704bd9\nauthor Abdul Rahman <81605929+abdulrahmancodes@users.noreply.github.com> 1789048453 +0000\ncommitter GitHub <noreply@github.com> 1789048453 +0000\n\nfeat(slack): answer the assistant request in the execution that records it (#25702)\n\n## Why\n\nIn production the Slack assistant can take minutes to answer, and the\nSlack Assistant Request record itself shows up late. The record is\ncreated by the queued enqueue step, so the time is lost in queue waits,\nnot in the assistant code. Until now a message went through three queued\nexecutions:\n\n1. the resolver, run inline by the server route;\n2. the enqueue function, one job on the logic function queue, which\ncreates the record;\n3. the worker, triggered by the record''s `created` event: one job on the\ntrigger queue (concurrency 1 per pod, fed by every record event of every\nworkspace), then another job on the logic function queue, waiting behind\nevery database-event trigger of every app.\n\n#25701 makes the route dispatch job in step 2 jump the fan-out. This PR\nremoves step 3 from the answer path.\n\n## What\n\n- `slack-events-enqueue` becomes `slack-assistant-request` (same\nuniversal identifier, so installs upgrade in place). It records the\nrequest as before and, when it has a resumable record, answers it in the\nsame execution. Its timeout is the worker''s 4 minute budget, and the\nagent deadline is computed at function entry so the failure-reply\nreserve is measured against the enclosing timeout.\n- The worker''s handler moves to\n`handlers/slack-assistant-worker-handler.ts` and takes the record\ndirectly instead of a database event.\n- `slack-assistant-worker` and its `created` trigger stay for one\nrelease as an upgrade drain. Events still waiting in the trigger backlog\nand worker jobs already emitted at upgrade time refer to that trigger\nand function; without them those requests would stay PENDING with no\nSlack redelivery. The drain hands the created record to the claim-based\nhandler, so a request already answered inline loses the claim and exits\nwith one cheap update. Remove the drain in the release after this one.\n- Retries resume unfinished work. A record found still PENDING is handed\nback to the worker rather than skipped, so an execution that died after\nwriting the record is recovered by the queue retry or a Slack\nredelivery. The loser of a racing create fetches the winning row and\nmakes the same decision.\n- A record killed mid-answer is recovered by a lease. A PROCESSING\nrecord whose `updatedAt` is older than the function timeout has no live\nowner (no execution outlives its timeout), so it is treated as\nresumable. The worker claims the record with one conditional update\n(`updateSlackAssistantRequests` filtered on `status = PENDING` or\n`status = PROCESSING and updatedAt < now - timeout`) and only proceeds\nif it won the row, so a redelivery racing a live execution can never\nproduce a second answer, while one that lands after the execution died\ntakes it over. The remaining window is a Slack post that succeeded\nwithout its DONE write followed by a retry more than 4 minutes later,\nwhich yields a duplicate answer rather than a lost one.\n- Trade-off made explicit: after #25701 the route dispatch job runs at\npriority 1, and this PR makes that job own the full 4 minute agent\nbudget rather than a short enqueue. Slack assistant runs therefore jump\nevery database-event trigger on the shared queue for the whole answer.\nThat is the intended choice while Slack volume is small next to the\nfan-out. The cleaner long-term seam is a \"user is waiting\" priority on\nthe SDK''s `enqueueJobs` or a separately concurrency-controlled\ninteractive lane on the server; `enqueueJobs` today enqueues at priority\n10, worse than the fan-out, which is why it was not used as the hand-off\nhere.\n- App version 1.0.2 to 1.1.0.\n\n## Test plan\n\n- `yarn typecheck`, `yarn lint` and `yarn test:unit` in\n`packages/twenty-apps/public/slack` are clean (687 tests, 101 files).\nUnit tests cover the request function''s hand-off and entry-time\ndeadline, the enqueue step''s create, resume, stale take-over,\ncreate-race and skip branches, the worker''s claim, lost-claim and lease\npaths, the lease predicate itself, and the drain''s delegation to the\nclaim-based handler.\n- The worker integration suite creates PENDING records so the claim runs\nagainst the live server; the enqueue integration tests assert the\nreturned record and the resume on redelivery. Both run in the\ntwenty-apps CI job.","reason":"valid","signature":"-----BEGIN PGP SIGNATURE-----\n\nwsFcBAABCAAQBQJqoraVCRC1aQ7uu5UhlAAAQG8QAAH0r4Zo9ghqekMy5X+hfFCF\n0KBrc3Tof6IT5rwL0K9G1vwuVSpXsrxlwkxtIkP1w8F/JaAVNNEp+xWdtYpjw/49\n3ZB3RuT8gGgb2N3azXGo9MnyKIjQhgZieOfd1R25ZyB8MQbP76L2LOCUAhD+/qqo\nV3m5Opz/REm0zQKFuZ+BcqUkV1xkGnKTkIY7NUBUzODpAguwZ9AN2t7+K00b/BtL\nCVzD8NAinyij8dZyWWF1KkfRDFQLA2kNRkOQj/RJJLAEunI/c7EfxeJg/APJhXxr\nnO07+goeXROBN8EdlKt1R6mKBoHy+vOKwHukVngJaVW6+AyNxEmH7UtpjRtV7m+Y\n81MZx4sWRCpAj6CITLAuW9e33WsIVpCrANHVtLejBznj6uFRcryYk4xEFPt9D2KL\n4eErycO9BFtOdQ0unSpZR2SmK86jDpiQgmlgHc00f+pJ1I8OdHtDTc3LdmToxB5a\ndCy8VuvOOEWoMzawt5UTctCGCfWf8VaImnQzGs4RVWZZWOHH0HkoyVaN9qv9XJ3r\n8z09enSMKt94sCP91WjCjBf2uDOQFzMoww8urNlZLBjjuFo7k+ZzDzUgGY6HM68X\nM8fgeMox4ob8aqqPAxxtSIR9sgFUouZcUIpEHRLr5i04qQ63jVCq8IJyeKFcQ50R\nGBT1Y8PkerFH4+N1HXyi\n=xuxm\n-----END PGP SIGNATURE-----\n","verified":true,"verified_at":"2026-09-12T09:06:20Z"}},"committer":{"avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/19864447?v=4","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/events{/privacy}","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"https://github.com/web-flow","id":19864447,"login":"web-flow","node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjE5ODY0NDQ3","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/orgs","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow","user_view_type":"public"},"html_url":"https://github.com/melon-1999/twenty/commit/1732736aa637835a0167cf4df2ae43727a789500","node_id":"C_kwDOTzfBtdoAKDE3MzI3MzZhYTYzNzgzNWEwMTY3Y2Y0ZGYyYWU0MzcyN2E3ODk1MDA","parents":[{"html_url":"https://github.com/melon-1999/twenty/commit/fa9870536abce97ef2cb9622d9d1941443704bd9","sha":"fa9870536abce97ef2cb9622d9d1941443704bd9","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/melon-1999/twenty/commits/fa9870536abce97ef2cb9622d9d1941443704bd9"}],"sha":"1732736aa637835a0167cf4df2ae43727a789500","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/melon-1999/twenty/commits/1732736aa637835a0167cf4df2ae43727a789500"},{"author":{"avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/81605929?v=4","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/abdulrahmancodes/events{/privacy}","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/abdulrahmancodes/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/abdulrahmancodes/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/abdulrahmancodes/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"https://github.com/abdulrahmancodes","id":81605929,"login":"abdulrahmancodes","node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjgxNjA1OTI5","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/abdulrahmancodes/orgs","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/abdulrahmancodes/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/abdulrahmancodes/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/abdulrahmancodes/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/abdulrahmancodes/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/abdulrahmancodes","user_view_type":"public"},"comments_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/melon-1999/twenty/commits/fa9870536abce97ef2cb9622d9d1941443704bd9/comments","commit":{"author":{"date":"2026-09-10T13:42:15Z","email":"81605929+abdulrahmancodes@users.noreply.github.com","name":"Abdul Rahman"},"comment_count":0,"committer":{"date":"2026-09-10T13:42:15Z","email":"noreply@github.com","name":"GitHub"},"message":"Show workspace-shared app connections to every member (#25708)\n\nThe application settings page listed app connections through\n`myConnectedAccounts`, which only returns rows the caller created. A\nconnection added as \"Workspace shared\" was usable by every member at\nruntime (the app-side `appConnections` query already respects\nvisibility) but only visible in settings to the person who created it.\nAfter #25598 every new connection is workspace shared, so everyone\nexcept the creator saw an empty table.\n\n## Changes\n\n- Server: new `applicationConnectedAccounts(applicationId)` query on\n`ConnectedAccountResolver`. It returns the non-archived app connections\nthe caller can use, workspace-shared ones from any member plus their own\npersonal ones, with an `isOwnedByCurrentUser` flag. The where-clause\ncomes from `buildConnectedAccountUsableByCallerWhere`, the SQL\ncounterpart of `isConnectedAccountUsableByCaller`. Rows are ordered by\n`createdAt` then `id`, like `findUsableByCaller`.\n- Front: `SettingsApplicationConnectionsSection` and\n`SettingsApplicationConnectionDetail` read from that query through\n`useApplicationConnectedAccounts(applicationId)`, which replaces\n`useMyAppConnectedAccounts`. The hook and the delete mutation''s refetch\nshare the generated `ApplicationConnectedAccountsDocument`, and the\nselection only carries the fields the two pages render.\n- Detail page: Reconnect, Share with workspace and Disconnect only\nrender for the owner. This is a UI choice only. The server''s\n`verifyOwnership` already lets any member act on a workspace-shared\nconnection and this PR does not change that.\n- Generated GraphQL clients (front metadata types and the client SDK\nmetadata client) regenerated against a running server.\n\n## Test\n\n- `npx jest src/pages/settings/applications\n--config=packages/twenty-front/jest.config.mjs` passes (13 tests),\nincluding cases for the owner and non-owner views of the detail page.\n- `npx jest\npackages/twenty-server/src/engine/metadata-modules/connected-account/utils\n--config=packages/twenty-server/jest.config.mjs` passes, including a\nspec for the where-clause util.\n- New `application-connected-accounts.integration-spec.ts` syncs an app\nwith an OAuth provider, inserts shared, private and archived connections\nowned by different members, and asserts the query returns the usable\nones with the right ownership flag.\n- `graphql:generate --configuration=metadata` and\n`twenty-client-sdk:generate-metadata-client` re-run against the server\nbuilt from this branch produce no further diff.\n- `tsgo --noEmit` clean on twenty-front and twenty-server.\n- oxlint and oxfmt clean on the changed files.","tree":{"sha":"6ff5daf6a49bf469b8e02a2698550aaec77bcefe","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/melon-1999/twenty/git/trees/6ff5daf6a49bf469b8e02a2698550aaec77bcefe"},"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/melon-1999/twenty/git/commits/fa9870536abce97ef2cb9622d9d1941443704bd9","verification":{"payload":"tree 6ff5daf6a49bf469b8e02a2698550aaec77bcefe\nparent b1701251d4aca0910f3ad0bcdb0532999600fecb\nauthor Abdul Rahman <81605929+abdulrahmancodes@users.noreply.github.com> 1789047735 +0000\ncommitter GitHub <noreply@github.com> 1789047735 +0000\n\nShow workspace-shared app connections to every member (#25708)\n\nThe application settings page listed app connections through\n`myConnectedAccounts`, which only returns rows the caller created. A\nconnection added as \"Workspace shared\" was usable by every member at\nruntime (the app-side `appConnections` query already respects\nvisibility) but only visible in settings to the person who created it.\nAfter #25598 every new connection is workspace shared, so everyone\nexcept the creator saw an empty table.\n\n## Changes\n\n- Server: new `applicationConnectedAccounts(applicationId)` query on\n`ConnectedAccountResolver`. It returns the non-archived app connections\nthe caller can use, workspace-shared ones from any member plus their own\npersonal ones, with an `isOwnedByCurrentUser` flag. The where-clause\ncomes from `buildConnectedAccountUsableByCallerWhere`, the SQL\ncounterpart of `isConnectedAccountUsableByCaller`. Rows are ordered by\n`createdAt` then `id`, like `findUsableByCaller`.\n- Front: `SettingsApplicationConnectionsSection` and\n`SettingsApplicationConnectionDetail` read from that query through\n`useApplicationConnectedAccounts(applicationId)`, which replaces\n`useMyAppConnectedAccounts`. The hook and the delete mutation''s refetch\nshare the generated `ApplicationConnectedAccountsDocument`, and the\nselection only carries the fields the two pages render.\n- Detail page: Reconnect, Share with workspace and Disconnect only\nrender for the owner. This is a UI choice only. The server''s\n`verifyOwnership` already lets any member act on a workspace-shared\nconnection and this PR does not change that.\n- Generated GraphQL clients (front metadata types and the client SDK\nmetadata client) regenerated against a running server.\n\n## Test\n\n- `npx jest src/pages/settings/applications\n--config=packages/twenty-front/jest.config.mjs` passes (13 tests),\nincluding cases for the owner and non-owner views of the detail page.\n- `npx jest\npackages/twenty-server/src/engine/metadata-modules/connected-account/utils\n--config=packages/twenty-server/jest.config.mjs` passes, including a\nspec for the where-clause util.\n- New `application-connected-accounts.integration-spec.ts` syncs an app\nwith an OAuth provider, inserts shared, private and archived connections\nowned by different members, and asserts the query returns the usable\nones with the right ownership flag.\n- `graphql:generate --configuration=metadata` and\n`twenty-client-sdk:generate-metadata-client` re-run against the server\nbuilt from this branch produce no further diff.\n- `tsgo --noEmit` clean on twenty-front and twenty-server.\n- oxlint and oxfmt clean on the changed files.","reason":"valid","signature":"-----BEGIN PGP SIGNATURE-----\n\nwsFcBAABCAAQBQJqorPtCRC1aQ7uu5UhlAAATxcQAIMLdOMn2EiJPfN6nrb5ARDL\n7c8QeKwsgIAGx37Xpdb+5DY3a6/ixX4GgaYNk4q3yg7ux1LmauYDC7uYO7anTE4e\nfSQswW2kwvuD9utf