github.com/users/web-flow/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow","user_view_type":"public"},"html_url":"https://github.com/melon-1999/twenty/commit/c3853a09591c36c9338e2242963cc33fc0b68f21","node_id":"C_kwDOTzfBtdoAKGMzODUzYTA5NTkxYzM2YzkzMzhlMjI0Mjk2M2NjMzNmYzBiNjhmMjE","parents":[{"html_url":"https://github.com/melon-1999/twenty/commit/38ba28ce23daaa596057f924d6d6ab6482155e71","sha":"38ba28ce23daaa596057f924d6d6ab6482155e71","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/melon-1999/twenty/commits/38ba28ce23daaa596057f924d6d6ab6482155e71"}],"sha":"c3853a09591c36c9338e2242963cc33fc0b68f21","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/melon-1999/twenty/commits/c3853a09591c36c9338e2242963cc33fc0b68f21"},{"author":{"avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/81605929?v=4","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/abdulrahmancodes/events{/privacy}","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/abdulrahmancodes/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/abdulrahmancodes/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/abdulrahmancodes/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"https://github.com/abdulrahmancodes","id":81605929,"login":"abdulrahmancodes","node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjgxNjA1OTI5","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/abdulrahmancodes/orgs","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/abdulrahmancodes/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/abdulrahmancodes/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/abdulrahmancodes/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/abdulrahmancodes/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/abdulrahmancodes","user_view_type":"public"},"comments_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/melon-1999/twenty/commits/38ba28ce23daaa596057f924d6d6ab6482155e71/comments","commit":{"author":{"date":"2026-09-10T05:13:59Z","email":"81605929+abdulrahmancodes@users.noreply.github.com","name":"Abdul Rahman"},"comment_count":0,"committer":{"date":"2026-09-10T05:13:59Z","email":"noreply@github.com","name":"GitHub"},"message":"feat(slack): defer rate limited message delivery to a delayed job (#25700)\n\nFollow-up to #25483, from @martmull''s review there: use `enqueueJobs`\nwith `delayMs` so a 429 does not make the calling function wait, and cap\nthe retries.\n\n**Stacked on #25483** (base is its branch, not `main`) because it relies\non `rejectRateLimitedCalls: true` landing there — without it a 429\nsleeps inside the client and never surfaces as an error to defer on.\n\n## What changed\n\nA 429 on a member-facing write now enqueues `slack-deliver-message` with\nthe delay Slack asked for, instead of the calling function spending its\nown budget waiting out the window.\n\n- **`slack-deliver-message`** posts one message and nothing else. On a\n429 it reschedules itself with `delayMs = retryAfter * 1000`; otherwise\nit settles.\n- **`enqueueSlackMessageDelivery`** wraps `enqueueJobs` and increments\nthe attempt counter.\n- **`SlackToolResult.retryAfterSeconds`**, set by `slackToolFailure` on\na rate-limit error, so the signal survives `postSlackMessage` turning\nexceptions into result objects.\n- **`slackAssistantWorker`** hands a rate-limited answer to the job and\nleaves the record `PROCESSING`; the job sets `DONE`/`FAILED`. Thread\nsubscribe and title still run on both paths.\n- **`sendSlackUserLinkConsentDm`** does the same, returning `{ success:\ntrue, deferred: true }`.\n\n## Two things the sketch could not have known\n\nBoth came out of reading the queue code, and both changed the shape:\n\n**`retryLimit` cannot bound a re-enqueue chain.** It maps to BullMQ\n`attempts: 1 + retryLimit` *within one job*\n([`bullmq.driver.ts:398`](https://github.com/twentyhq/twenty/blob/main/packages/twenty-server/src/engine/core-modules/message-queue/drivers/bullmq.driver.ts#L398)).\nA rescheduled delivery is a fresh job with fresh attempts, so the cap\nhas to travel in the payload — hence `attempt` and\n`SLACK_MESSAGE_DELIVERY_MAX_ATTEMPTS`.\n\n**The handler never sees the attempt count.**\n`ApplicationJobService.enqueueJobs` passes only `{ logicFunctionId,\nworkspaceId, payload, userId?, userWorkspaceId? }`. So a job that simply\nthrows cannot tell it is on its last try — which matters here, because\nsomething has to move the assistant request out of `PROCESSING`. That is\nwhy the handler catches the 429 itself rather than throwing.\n\nWorth noting either way: logic function jobs already get a server-side\nexponential backoff (`LOGIC_FUNCTION_QUEUE_RETRY_BACKOFF`, 1s initial,\njitter 0.5), but it ignores Slack''s `Retry-After`, so the delay has to\nbe set explicitly.\n\n## Scope\n\nOnly the two fire-and-forget senders move: the assistant answer and the\nconsent DM. Neither has an ordering dependency.\n\n`postSlackChannelWelcome` is deliberately left alone. It posts a thread\nreply using the first post''s `slackTs`, so deferring the first breaks\nthe second — the delivery job would have to enqueue its own follow-up,\nwhich is the part worth reviewing on its own rather than smuggling in\nhere.\n\n`retrySlackCallWhenRateLimited` therefore stays in `postSlackMessage`:\nchannel-welcome and `replyToEmptySlackAssistantRequest` still depend on\nit and have no deferral path yet. The budget arithmetic goes away once\nthe remaining callers migrate.\n\n## Tests\n\n8 new unit tests across 3 files: delivery marking the request done,\nrescheduling at the delay Slack asked for, stopping at the attempt cap,\nthe no-assistant-request path, attempt counting across the chain, and\nthe `Retry-After` surviving into the result.\n\n- `yarn test:unit`: 647 passed, 93 files (639 / 91 on the base branch)\n- `yarn lint` and typecheck clean; `oxfmt --check` clean on all 15\nchanged files\n- Verified the attempt-cap test fails without its guard\n- The integration test''s `{ done: true }` assertion is unaffected — its\nmsw mocks never return a 429\n\n## Gaps\n\n- No worker-level test for the deferral branch. The worker has no unit\nharness today, only an integration test; the branch is three lines and\nthe job it enqueues is covered, but not at that layer.\n- Nothing here has run against a live stack. `enqueueJobs` from inside a\nlogic function, and `getSlackClient()` resolving in a job context, are\nreasoned from the server source rather than observed.\n\n<!-- This is an auto-generated description by cubic. -->\n<a\nhref=\"https://cubic.dev/pr/twentyhq/twenty/pull/25700?utm_source=github\"\ntarget=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"\ndata-no-image-dialog=\"true\"><picture>``&lt;source\nmedia=\"(prefers-color-scheme: dark)\"\nsrcset=\"https://www.cubic.dev/buttons/review-in-cubic-dark.svg\"&gt;&lt;source\nmedia=\"(prefers-color-scheme: light)\"\nsrcset=\"https://www.cubic.dev/buttons/review-in-cubic-light.svg\"&gt;&lt;img\nalt=\"Review in cubic\"\nsrc=\"https://www.cubic.dev/buttons/review-in-cubic-dark.svg\"&gt;``</picture></a>\n<!-- End of auto-generated description by cubic. -->","tree":{"sha":"64997193b99456ae2e20f8e4a52f3a712e3c098c","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/melon-1999/twenty/git/trees/64997193b99456ae2e20f8e4a52f3a712e3c098c"},"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/melon-1999/twenty/git/commits/38ba28ce23daaa596057f924d6d6ab6482155e71","verification":{"payload":"tree 64997193b99456ae2e20f8e4a52f3a712e3c098c\nparent 88fe4360fea326fb8998a15c07db1bd003a9fe0d\nauthor Abdul Rahman <81605929+abdulrahmancodes@users.noreply.github.com> 1789017239 +0000\ncommitter GitHub <noreply@github.com> 1789017239 +0000\n\nfeat(slack): defer rate limited message delivery to a delayed job (#25700)\n\nFollow-up to #25483, from @martmull''s review there: use `enqueueJobs`\nwith `delayMs` so a 429 does not make the calling function wait, and cap\nthe retries.\n\n**Stacked on #25483** (base is its branch, not `main`) because it relies\non `rejectRateLimitedCalls: true` landing there — without it a 429\nsleeps inside the client and never surfaces as an error to defer on.\n\n## What changed\n\nA 429 on a member-facing write now enqueues `slack-deliver-message` with\nthe delay Slack asked for, instead of the calling function spending its\nown budget waiting out the window.\n\n- **`slack-deliver-message`** posts one message and nothing else. On a\n429 it reschedules itself with `delayMs = retryAfter * 1000`; otherwise\nit settles.\n- **`enqueueSlackMessageDelivery`** wraps `enqueueJobs` and increments\nthe attempt counter.\n- **`SlackToolResult.retryAfterSeconds`**, set by `slackToolFailure` on\na rate-limit error, so the signal survives `postSlackMessage` turning\nexceptions into result objects.\n- **`slackAssistantWorker`** hands a rate-limited answer to the job and\nleaves the record `PROCESSING`; the job sets `DONE`/`FAILED`. Thread\nsubscribe and title still run on both paths.\n- **`sendSlackUserLinkConsentDm`** does the same, returning `{ success:\ntrue, deferred: true }`.\n\n## Two things the sketch could not have known\n\nBoth came out of reading the queue code, and both changed the shape:\n\n**`retryLimit` cannot bound a re-enqueue chain.** It maps to BullMQ\n`attempts: 1 + retryLimit` *within one job*\n([`bullmq.driver.ts:398`](https://github.com/twentyhq/twenty/blob/main/packages/twenty-server/src/engine/core-modules/message-queue/drivers/bullmq.driver.ts#L398)).\nA rescheduled delivery is a fresh job with fresh attempts, so the cap\nhas to travel in the payload — hence `attempt` and\n`SLACK_MESSAGE_DELIVERY_MAX_ATTEMPTS`.\n\n**The handler never sees the attempt count.**\n`ApplicationJobService.enqueueJobs` passes only `{ logicFunctionId,\nworkspaceId, payload, userId?, userWorkspaceId? }`. So a job that simply\nthrows cannot tell it is on its last try — which matters here, because\nsomething has to move the assistant request out of `PROCESSING`. That is\nwhy the handler catches the 429 itself rather than throwing.\n\nWorth noting either way: logic function jobs already get a server-side\nexponential backoff (`LOGIC_FUNCTION_QUEUE_RETRY_BACKOFF`, 1s initial,\njitter 0.5), but it ignores Slack''s `Retry-After`, so the delay has to\nbe set explicitly.\n\n## Scope\n\nOnly the two fire-and-forget senders move: the assistant answer and the\nconsent DM. Neither has an ordering dependency.\n\n`postSlackChannelWelcome` is deliberately left alone. It posts a thread\nreply using the first post''s `slackTs`, so deferring the first breaks\nthe second — the delivery job would have to enqueue its own follow-up,\nwhich is the part worth reviewing on its own rather than smuggling in\nhere.\n\n`retrySlackCallWhenRateLimited` therefore stays in `postSlackMessage`:\nchannel-welcome and `replyToEmptySlackAssistantRequest` still depend on\nit and have no deferral path yet. The budget arithmetic goes away once\nthe remaining callers migrate.\n\n## Tests\n\n8 new unit tests across 3 files: delivery marking the request done,\nrescheduling at the delay Slack asked for, stopping at the attempt cap,\nthe no-assistant-request path, attempt counting across the chain, and\nthe `Retry-After` surviving into the result.\n\n- `yarn test:unit`: 647 passed, 93 files (639 / 91 on the base branch)\n- `yarn lint` and typecheck clean; `oxfmt --check` clean on all 15\nchanged files\n- Verified the attempt-cap test fails without its guard\n- The integration test''s `{ done: true }` assertion is unaffected — its\nmsw mocks never return a 429\n\n## Gaps\n\n- No worker-level test for the deferral branch. The worker has no unit\nharness today, only an integration test; the branch is three lines and\nthe job it enqueues is covered, but not at that layer.\n- Nothing here has run against a live stack. `enqueueJobs` from inside a\nlogic function, and `getSlackClient()` resolving in a job context, are\nreasoned from the server source rather than observed.\n\n<!-- This is an auto-generated description by cubic. -->\n<a\nhref=\"https://cubic.dev/pr/twentyhq/twenty/pull/25700?utm_source=github\"\ntarget=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"\ndata-no-image-dialog=\"true\"><picture>``&lt;source\nmedia=\"(prefers-color-scheme: dark)\"\nsrcset=\"https://www.cubic.dev/buttons/review-in-cubic-dark.svg\"&gt;&lt;source\nmedia=\"(prefers-color-scheme: light)\"\nsrcset=\"https://www.cubic.dev/buttons/review-in-cubic-light.svg\"&gt;&lt;img\nalt=\"Review in cubic\"\nsrc=\"https://www.cubic.dev/buttons/review-in-cubic-dark.svg\"&gt;``</picture></a>\n<!-- End of auto-generated description by cubic. -->","reason":"valid","signature":"-----BEGIN PGP SIGNATURE-----\n\nwsFcBAABCAAQBQJqojynCRC1aQ7uu5UhlAAAuy4QAApPyZ9c2MR8YTAtJuy7dFw1\nbPoegz8G98ZCK7rlII1at4yvt5dpbQ7KmQnNLmpPK0wi2c1E8AX9C8NhnYs7MvwJ\ndZIyszy2HVo5eh+NlB4KyyNrm5+FhPI/PKHv902d3KozyxvOqKcc6f3CxUCPMPTp\niYtFy4De1GGDQKelJ2yDoakoLFQFUUwMi/uAy1OxH6GfYPXnXCsH49qSxyhjSa5C\nE+uy8D4669MvRT1ebus+SLZxi5jMDGpKy7SEJP8L9jMFYo5EEqTmL0ko7vFwUDn6\nbE71W652LpDX4ZZWykLz6zhZE2Ds3VCctGa8NhbJ3i9ARnnAHlRGgBgXYggN9dtn\n+twfvtu8edScXXVkhb6kuooVRL/sAvaPl2uWd2unXiTYBZrEkBslzjokQjStDgfX\nlk1bNbzEpk2GTHAt/as6P7e6hdCg/BicJ45eFDv0OhXyZq/Ll36soKBFmTxvLlAS\nwTNgMrakFRKikTnKX73HZ0nLzAIITW3zmcVJnjT1FuA6yeiUbiJL7jgEDmXnybUl\nk5A4RjEUNy2cjs7zu6XcLF2PyGIth8XqHhyIYbFCl855xdjjiPJJkG1Ff4OEL7YS\nI2YLHwm/4rKRDJdwyiyrZfXOB/nnv6305oRoz84m8kXY1U8CJS8ur9U/3W7EtOyX\ngRwZC05F0pdikmj5qjvd\n=rKjK\n-----END PGP SIGNATURE-----\n","verified":true,"verified_at":"2026-09-12T09:06:20Z"}},"committer":{"avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/19864447?v=4","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/events{/privacy}","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"https://github.com/web-flow","id":19864447,"login":"web-flow","node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjE5ODY0NDQ3","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/orgs","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow","user_view_type":"public"},"html_url":"https://github.com/melon-1999/twenty/commit/38ba28ce23daaa596057f924d6d6ab6482155e71","node_id":"C_kwDOTzfBtdoAKDM4YmEyOGNlMjNkYWFhNTk2MDU3ZjkyNGQ2ZDZhYjY0ODIxNTVlNzE","parents":[{"html_url":"https://github.com/melon-1999/twenty/commit/88fe4360fea326fb8998a15c07db1bd003a9fe0d","sha":"88fe4360fea326fb8998a15c07db1bd003a9fe0d","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/melon-1999/twenty/commits/88fe4360fea326fb8998a15c07db1bd003a9fe0d"}],"sha":"38ba28ce23daaa596057f924d6d6ab6482155e71","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/melon-1999/twenty/commits/38ba28ce23daaa596057f924d6d6ab6482155e71"},{"author":{"avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/81605929?v=4","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/abdulrahmancodes/events{/privacy}","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/abdulrahmancodes/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/abdulrahmancodes/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/abdulrahmancodes/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"https://github.com/abdulrahmancodes","id":81605929,"login":"abdulrahmancodes","node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjgxNjA1OTI5","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/abdulrahmancodes/orgs","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/abdulrahmancodes/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/abdulrahmancodes/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/abdulrahmancodes/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/abdulrahmancodes/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/abdulrahmancodes","user_view_type":"public"},"comments_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/melon-1999/twenty/commits/88fe4360fea326fb8998a15c07db1bd003a9fe0d/comments","commit":{"author":{"date":"2026-09-10T04:58:36Z","email":"81605929+abdulrahmancodes@users.noreply.github.com","name":"Abdul Rahman"},"comment_count":0,"committer":{"date":"2026-09-10T04:58:36Z","email":"noreply@github.com","name":"GitHub"},"message":"fix(slack): bound Slack Web API retries to the logic function budget (#25483)\n\n## Problem\n\n`getSlackClient` built every `WebClient` with the `@slack/web-api`\ndefaults. Confirmed against the installed 7.19.0 dist, not the docs:\n\n- `retryConfig` defaults to `tenRetriesInAboutThirtyMinutes` (`{\nretries: 10, factor: 1.96821, randomize: true }`)\n- `timeout` defaults to `0`, so there is no per-request timeout at all\n\nMeanwhile the logic functions calling it have hard budgets: 15s for the\nroute functions, 30-120s for the tool and settings functions, 240s for\nthe assistant worker. A rate-limited Slack call therefore did not fail,\nit hung until the function was killed.\n\nTwo details make it worse than it first looks:\n\n- On a 429 the client sleeps the full `Retry-After` and *then* throws a\nretryable error, so the header wait and the p-retry backoff stack.\n`retryConfig: { retries: 0 }` does not bound this, since the sleep\nhappens before the retry budget applies. Only `rejectRateLimitedCalls`\navoids it.\n- With `timeout: 0`, a Slack call that never answers never returns.\n\n19 of the 21 `getSlackClient` call sites inherited these defaults. Only\n`runBestEffortSlackCall` (assistant status/title) and the connection\nstatus handler bounded them.\n\nThe sharpest case is the assistant worker. `fetchSlackAssistantContext`\nsits in a `Promise.all` outside `raceSlackAssistantAgentDeadline`, which\nraces only the agent run, while `agentDeadlineAtMs` is already ticking.\nA slow context read silently spends the agent budget, and past 240s the\nfunction is hard-killed before any failure text is posted: the member\nsees nothing and the request record stays `PROCESSING`.\n\nThis is not a volume question. `conversations.replies` is rate limited\nto **1 request per minute** for apps distributed outside the Slack\nMarketplace (since 2025-05-29), and `fetchSlackThreadMessages` walks up\nto 10 pages sequentially per assistant turn, so the second page 429s\ndeterministically on such an install. `chat.postMessage` is also\nplausibly hit at its ~1/s per channel limit.\n\nA Slack Marketplace listing is in progress for the Twenty Cloud app,\nwhich would lift that specific cap on Cloud. It does not remove the need\nfor this change. Per\n[SETUP.md](https://github.com/twentyhq/twenty/blob/main/packages/twenty-apps/public/slack/SETUP.md),\nTwenty connects to a Slack app the admin creates and owns, so every\nself-hosted install stays on the unlisted 1/min limit. And the defect is\nnot rate-limit specific: with `timeout: 0` a request that never answers\nnever returns, and `chat.postMessage` is ~1/s per channel for listed and\nunlisted apps alike.\n\n## Changes\n\n- **`get-slack-client.ts`** now defaults to a 3s request timeout, one\nshort retry (enough for a transient 5xx), and `rejectRateLimitedCalls:\ntrue` so a 429 surfaces immediately instead of sleeping. Callers can\nstill override any of it.\n- **`retrySlackCallWhenRateLimited`** bounds the whole operation, not\njust the sleep: it charges the first attempt against a budget, so a\nretry only runs when `elapsed + Retry-After` fits. A rate-limited call\nreturns immediately, so a legitimate retry still happens; the retry is\ndropped only when Slack was slow as well, which is exactly when there is\nno budget for it. The budget is a parameter defaulting to\n`SLACK_RATE_LIMIT_RETRY_BUDGET_MS` (4s), so callers with more room can\nraise it.\n- **Member-facing writes get that retry**: `chat.postMessage` in\n`post-slack-message.ts` and the consent DM. Failing fast everywhere\nwould be *worse* than before for the member, since a 429 on delivery\nwould drop the answer and the failure text with it. `chat.update`,\n`chat.delete` and `chat.unfurl` deliberately fail fast — the agent sees\nthe tool result, and unfurls are cosmetic.\n- **The roster match waits out a rate limit within its budget.**\n`users.list` is Tier 2 (20/min) and `collectSlackRosterMembers` pages up\nto five times inside a 120s function, so it passes\n`SLACK_ROSTER_RATE_LIMIT_RETRY_BUDGET_MS` (60s). The deadline is\ncomputed once before the paging loop and each page gets the remaining\nslice, so five rate-limited pages cannot wait 60s each.\n- **`fetchSlackAssistantContext`** bounds its whole read path at 30s via\na new `runWithTimeout` helper. Client acquisition and the thread reads\nare bounded separately but chained off a single `contextDeadlineAtMs`,\nso the two stages cannot spend 30s each. On timeout it falls back to an\nempty context that keeps the acquired `WebClient`, which\n`resolveSlackRunAsForRequest` still needs; answer delivery acquires its\nown client through `slackPostMessageHandler` and is unaffected either\nway. Those reads already degrade to \"answer without thread history\" when\nthey throw, so this reuses existing behaviour rather than adding a new\nfailure mode.\n- **The context read gets a 10s request timeout**\n(`SLACK_ASSISTANT_CONTEXT_REQUEST_TIMEOUT_MS`) rather than the 3s\ndefault, passed through the caller override so nothing else is loosened.\nA thread page asks Slack for up to 1000 replies, which 3s can cut short\n— and because `fetchSlackThreadMessages` swallows the error, that would\nsilently drop thread history on exactly the large threads where it\nmatters.\n- **`raceSlackAssistantAgentDeadline`** now delegates to\n`runWithTimeout` so the package carries one timeout seam rather than\ntwo.\n\nNet effect for the member: today a rate-limited worker is killed\nsilently; now it answers, possibly without thread history.\n\n## Known limitation\n\n`postSlackChannelWelcome` posts twice sequentially inside a 15s\nfunction, so its pathological worst case (~20.5s) still exceeds that\nbudget. It needs repeated request timeouts *and* rate limiting at once;\nthe realistic rate-limited case resolves in ~2s per post, since\n`chat.postMessage` is ~1/s per channel and a `Retry-After: 1` is waited\nout inside the budget. Closing the pathological case means raising that\nfunction''s `timeoutSeconds` or making the thread reply best-effort, both\nbehaviour changes to a function this PR otherwise does not touch, so it\nis left as a documented gap. Pre-PR that same path could hang for ~30\nminutes per post.\n\n`runWithTimeout` bounds caller latency without cancelling the losing\noperation, matching the existing deadline race''s semantics. The\nabandoned work is capped by the client and its result discarded; real\ncancellation would mean threading an `AbortSignal` through four utils.\n\n## Tests\n\n23 new unit tests across 6 files, covering the client defaults and\ncaller override, the rate-limit retry budget (including a first attempt\nthat has already spent it), the shared roster window across pages, the\ntimeout helper, the bounded context read (a hanging client acquisition\nand the shared deadline), and rate-limited answer delivery.\n\n- `yarn test:unit`: 639 passed, 91 files\n- `yarn lint` and `yarn typecheck` clean; `oxfmt --check` clean on all\n22 changed files\n- `yarn test` (integration) needs a live Twenty at `localhost:2020` and\nwas not run locally. It runs in CI as the `slack (local)` and `slack\n(dockerhub-latest)` jobs. Those tests mock Slack over msw with HTTP 200\n+ `ok: false` and never produce a 429 or a hang, so this change is inert\nfor them.","tree":{"sha":"dbdd01e677da30d99955114cfc37f564d02dae87","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/melon-1999/twenty/git/trees/dbdd01e677da30d99955114cfc37f564d02dae87"},"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/melon-1999/twenty/git/commits/88fe4360fea326fb8998a15c07db1bd003a9fe0d","verification":{"payload":"tree dbdd01e677da30d99955114cfc37f564d02dae87\nparent a4f14ce193406f6d4a72e4be30e43f173d86f198\nauthor Abdul Rahman <81605929+abdulrahmancodes@users.noreply.github.com> 1789016316 +0000\ncommitter GitHub <noreply@github.com> 1789016316 +0000\n\nfix(slack): bound Slack Web API retries to the logic function budget (#25483)\n\n## Problem\n\n`getSlackClient` built every `WebClient` with the `@slack/web-api`\ndefaults. Confirmed against the installed 7.19.0 dist, not the docs:\n\n- `retryConfig` defaults to `tenRetriesInAboutThirtyMinutes` (`{\nretries: 10, factor: 1.96821, randomize: true }`)\n- `timeout` defaults to `0`, so there is no per-request timeout at all\n\nMeanwhile the logic functions calling it have hard budgets: 15s for the\nroute functions, 30-120s for the tool and settings functions, 240s for\nthe assistant worker. A rate-limited Slack call therefore did not fail,\nit hung until the function was killed.\n\nTwo details make it worse than it first looks:\n\n- On a 429 the client sleeps the full `Retry-After` and *then* throws a\nretryable error, so the header wait and the p-retry backoff stack.\n`retryConfig: { retries: 0 }` does not bound this, since the sleep\nhappens before the retry budget applies. Only `rejectRateLimitedCalls`\navoids it.\n- With `timeout: 0`, a Slack call that never answers never returns.\n\n19 of the 21 `getSlackClient` call sites inherited these defaults. Only\n`runBestEffortSlackCall` (assistant status/title) and the connection\nstatus handler bounded them.\n\nThe sharpest case is the assistant worker. `fetchSlackAssistantContext`\nsits in a `Promise.all` outside `raceSlackAssistantAgentDeadline`, which\nraces only the agent run, while `agentDeadlineAtMs` is already ticking.\nA slow context read silently spends the agent budget, and past 240s the\nfunction is hard-killed before any failure text is posted: the member\nsees nothing and the request record stays `PROCESSING`.\n\nThis is not a volume question. `conversations.replies` is rate limited\nto **1 request per minute** for apps distributed outside the Slack\nMarketplace (since 2025-05-29), and `fetchSlackThreadMessages` walks up\nto 10 pages sequentially per assistant turn, so the second page 429s\ndeterministically on such an install. `chat.postMessage` is also\nplausibly hit at its ~1/s per channel limit.\n\nA Slack Marketplace listing is in progress for the Twenty Cloud app,\nwhich would lift that specific cap on Cloud. It does not remove the need\nfor this change. Per\n[SETUP.md](https://github.com/twentyhq/twenty/blob/main/packages/twenty-apps/public/slack/SETUP.md),\nTwenty connects to a Slack app the admin creates and owns, so every\nself-hosted install stays on the unlisted 1/min limit. And the defect is\nnot rate-limit specific: with `timeout: 0` a request that never answers\nnever returns, and `chat.postMessage` is ~1/s per channel for listed and\nunlisted apps alike.\n\n## Changes\n\n- **`get-slack-client.ts`** now defaults to a 3s request timeout, one\nshort retry (enough for a transient 5xx), and `rejectRateLimitedCalls:\ntrue` so a 429 surfaces immediately instead of sleeping. Callers can\nstill override any of it.\n- **`retrySlackCallWhenRateLimited`** bounds the whole operation, not\njust the sleep: it charges the first attempt against a budget, so a\nretry only runs when `elapsed + Retry-After` fits. A rate-limited call\nreturns immediately, so a legitimate retry still happens; the retry is\ndropped only when Slack was slow as well, which is exactly when there is\nno budget for it. The budget is a parameter defaulting to\n`SLACK_RATE_LIMIT_RETRY_BUDGET_MS` (4s), so callers with more room can\nraise it.\n- **Member-facing writes get that retry**: `chat.postMessage` in\n`post-slack-message.ts` and the consent DM. Failing fast